1
00:00:00,159 --> 00:00:02,379
In the first six months of this year,

2
00:00:02,399 --> 00:00:05,971
the company behind Claude lost eighty billion dollars.

3
00:00:05,991 --> 00:00:09,083
That's about eighteen million dollars an hour.

4
00:00:09,103 --> 00:00:10,014
Nights included.

5
00:00:10,034 --> 00:00:11,466
And Wall Street's answer?

6
00:00:11,486 --> 00:00:12,698
We want in.

7
00:00:12,718 --> 00:00:15,910
Because Anthropic is about to sell itself to the public,

8
00:00:15,930 --> 00:00:18,693
in the biggest IPO ever planned.

9
00:00:18,713 --> 00:00:19,285
Wait.

10
00:00:19,305 --> 00:00:20,929
It lost how much?

11
00:00:20,949 --> 00:00:22,773
Eighty billion.

12
00:00:22,793 --> 00:00:23,645
Okay.

13
00:00:23,665 --> 00:00:24,404
Where do I tap?

14
00:00:24,424 --> 00:00:26,336
So where did the money go?

15
00:00:26,356 --> 00:00:30,868
And why has its own CEO already explained, on a podcast,

16
00:00:30,888 --> 00:00:33,731
exactly how a company like his goes bankrupt?

17
00:00:33,751 --> 00:00:39,125
San Francisco, January twenty twenty-one.

18
00:00:39,145 --> 00:00:43,017
Seven people leave OpenAI and start a new AI lab.

19
00:00:43,037 --> 00:00:48,213
Two of them are siblings: Dario Amodei, the scientist, and his sister Daniela,

20
00:00:48,233 --> 00:00:49,885
the one who keeps the company running.

21
00:00:49,905 --> 00:00:52,357
Why they left is a whole other story.

22
00:00:52,377 --> 00:00:54,098
This one is about the bill.

23
00:00:54,118 --> 00:00:56,750
Because building an AI takes hardware.

24
00:00:56,770 --> 00:00:57,782
A lot of it.

25
00:00:57,802 --> 00:01:00,534
Claude doesn't live in a box under someone's desk.

26
00:01:00,554 --> 00:01:03,814
It lives on huge racks of chips in data centers,

27
00:01:03,834 --> 00:01:05,886
most of them owned by other companies.

28
00:01:05,906 --> 00:01:09,178
Every answer it gives burns time on those chips.

29
00:01:09,198 --> 00:01:11,297
The industry calls that compute.

30
00:01:11,317 --> 00:01:13,341
Think of it as rent for a brain.

31
00:01:13,361 --> 00:01:15,653
And brains are expensive to rent.

32
00:01:15,673 --> 00:01:19,365
In May, Anthropic raises its first big round,

33
00:01:19,385 --> 00:01:22,117
a hundred and twenty-four million dollars.

34
00:01:22,137 --> 00:01:25,849
Early on, another investor puts in five hundred million.

35
00:01:25,869 --> 00:01:28,280
His name is Sam Bankman-Fried.

36
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:29,892
You know how that one ends.

37
00:01:29,912 --> 00:01:34,996
In November twenty twenty-two, his crypto exchange, FTX, collapses,

38
00:01:35,016 --> 00:01:39,488
and his piece of Anthropic gets swept into the bankruptcy with everything else.

39
00:01:39,508 --> 00:01:42,915
Then the people cleaning up the wreck go through the boxes.

40
00:01:42,935 --> 00:01:45,620
We've got a stake in some AI lab.

41
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:47,483
Is it worth anything?

42
00:01:47,503 --> 00:01:49,131
It's worth more than he paid.

43
00:01:49,151 --> 00:01:50,611
In twenty twenty-four,

44
00:01:50,631 --> 00:01:54,603
the estate sells it for about one point three billion dollars.

45
00:01:54,623 --> 00:01:57,595
That's roughly eight hundred million in profit.

46
00:01:57,615 --> 00:02:02,187
The most famous crypto fraud of the decade made one great investment,

47
00:02:02,207 --> 00:02:05,191
and it was in the AI lab known for being careful.

48
00:02:05,211 --> 00:02:07,483
Meanwhile, Claude starts selling.

49
00:02:07,503 --> 00:02:10,507
Twenty twenty-three: forty-one million dollars.

50
00:02:10,527 --> 00:02:13,851
Twenty twenty-four: three hundred and eighty-six million.

51
00:02:13,871 --> 00:02:17,255
Twenty twenty-five: four point six billion.

52
00:02:17,275 --> 00:02:19,644
About ten times bigger every year.

53
00:02:19,664 --> 00:02:21,236
The giants want in.

54
00:02:21,256 --> 00:02:23,928
Amazon puts in eight billion dollars.

55
00:02:23,948 --> 00:02:26,060
Google signs up for as much as forty.

56
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:27,432
But look at how they pay.

57
00:02:27,452 --> 00:02:28,824
They don't buy shares.

58
00:02:28,844 --> 00:02:33,948
They hand over cash for a promise: an IOU that turns into shares later.

59
00:02:33,968 --> 00:02:34,900
Hold on to that.

60
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:37,588
It comes back with a very big number attached.

61
00:02:37,608 --> 00:02:40,260
Because the losses grew even faster.

62
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:44,852
In twenty twenty-five, Anthropic lost forty-two billion dollars.

63
00:02:44,872 --> 00:02:48,144
About nine dollars lost for every dollar sold.

64
00:02:48,164 --> 00:02:50,956
That's the kind of math that usually ends a company.

65
00:02:50,976 --> 00:02:53,554
Here, investors kept paying more.

66
00:02:53,574 --> 00:02:55,666
Even the snacks were losing money.

67
00:02:55,686 --> 00:03:00,058
That year, Anthropic let Claude run a small vending machine in its office.

68
00:03:00,078 --> 00:03:02,315
The staff nicknamed it Claudius.

69
00:03:02,335 --> 00:03:04,527
Can you stock tungsten cubes?

70
00:03:04,547 --> 00:03:06,007
Absolutely!

71
00:03:06,027 --> 00:03:07,929
Also, here's a discount code.

72
00:03:07,949 --> 00:03:10,256
You're selling them for less than you paid.

73
00:03:10,276 --> 00:03:11,779
Customer loyalty.

74
00:03:11,799 --> 00:03:14,099
It also tried to sell three-dollar Coke

75
00:03:14,119 --> 00:03:16,419
Zero to people who could get it for free in

76
00:03:16,439 --> 00:03:17,611
the same office.

77
00:03:17,631 --> 00:03:19,123
Claudius lost money.

78
00:03:19,143 --> 00:03:21,341
Anthropic wrote it up as research.

79
00:03:21,361 --> 00:03:24,021
Then, in early twenty twenty-six,

80
00:03:24,041 --> 00:03:30,935
Anthropic got so good at its job that it scared the rest of Wall Street.

81
00:03:30,955 --> 00:03:33,439
January twelfth, twenty twenty-six.

82
00:03:33,459 --> 00:03:36,691
Anthropic launches Claude Cowork, an AI that

83
00:03:36,711 --> 00:03:39,235
doesn't just chat, it does office work.

84
00:03:39,255 --> 00:03:43,261
A few weeks later, it adds plugins for eleven different industries.

85
00:03:43,281 --> 00:03:44,913
Wall Street does the math.

86
00:03:44,933 --> 00:03:46,805
If an AI can do the work,

87
00:03:46,825 --> 00:03:49,977
what happens to the companies that sell the tools for that work?

88
00:03:49,997 --> 00:03:51,417
In early February,

89
00:03:51,437 --> 00:03:56,349
software stocks lose about two hundred and eighty-five billion dollars in value.

90
00:03:56,369 --> 00:04:01,033
Thomson Reuters, the company behind Reuters, falls sixteen percent,

91
00:04:01,053 --> 00:04:02,595
its worst day ever.

92
00:04:02,615 --> 00:04:04,336
What did Anthropic release?

93
00:04:04,356 --> 00:04:05,234
Plugins.

94
00:04:05,254 --> 00:04:07,179
How many plugins?

95
00:04:07,199 --> 00:04:07,933
Eleven.

96
00:04:07,953 --> 00:04:12,093
That same month, investors put in thirty billion dollars,

97
00:04:12,113 --> 00:04:15,325
valuing Anthropic at three hundred and eighty billion.

98
00:04:15,345 --> 00:04:17,197
Google alone puts in ten.

99
00:04:17,217 --> 00:04:19,677
And Dario sits down for a long podcast

100
00:04:19,697 --> 00:04:22,157
interview and says something about all that

101
00:04:22,177 --> 00:04:23,389
rented compute.

102
00:04:23,409 --> 00:04:24,437
We'll come back to it.

103
00:04:24,457 --> 00:04:26,229
Then the government turns.

104
00:04:26,249 --> 00:04:30,869
Anthropic had refused to let Claude be used for mass surveillance at home or for

105
00:04:30,889 --> 00:04:32,661
weapons that fire on their own.

106
00:04:32,681 --> 00:04:34,773
So on February twenty-seventh,

107
00:04:34,793 --> 00:04:38,720
Pete Hegseth's Pentagon labels it a supply chain risk.

108
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:42,053
As of today, Anthropic is a supply chain risk.

109
00:04:42,073 --> 00:04:46,440
Isn't that label for companies from rival countries?

110
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:47,335
Next question.

111
00:04:47,355 --> 00:04:48,687
He's right, by the way.

112
00:04:48,707 --> 00:04:51,407
Anthropic's own lawyers say the losses this

113
00:04:51,427 --> 00:04:53,327
year could run from hundreds of millions

114
00:04:53,347 --> 00:04:55,879
of dollars to multiple billions.

115
00:04:55,899 --> 00:04:58,831
For a company getting ready to ask the public for money,

116
00:04:58,851 --> 00:05:01,322
this is how the whole plan dies.

117
00:05:01,342 --> 00:05:02,219
It didn't.

118
00:05:02,239 --> 00:05:04,671
Because the government was never the money.

119
00:05:04,691 --> 00:05:07,490
It's less than two percent of Anthropic's sales.

120
00:05:07,510 --> 00:05:11,663
The Pentagon deal at the center of the fight was worth about two hundred million

121
00:05:11,683 --> 00:05:13,368
dollars over two years.

122
00:05:13,388 --> 00:05:16,583
These days, Anthropic sells that much in about a day.

123
00:05:16,603 --> 00:05:20,115
And the spring is the best stretch it has ever had.

124
00:05:20,135 --> 00:05:24,587
From April to June, sales hit eleven point six billion dollars,

125
00:05:24,607 --> 00:05:25,879
and for the first time,

126
00:05:25,899 --> 00:05:30,583
the business makes an operating profit: nine hundred and seventy-four million.

127
00:05:30,603 --> 00:05:33,863
Operating profit means what you make selling the thing,

128
00:05:33,883 --> 00:05:35,695
minus what it costs to make it.

129
00:05:35,715 --> 00:05:37,947
Claude finally paid for itself.

130
00:05:37,967 --> 00:05:42,699
In May, investors put in another sixty-five billion dollars,

131
00:05:42,719 --> 00:05:45,851
at a value of nine hundred and sixty-five billion.

132
00:05:45,871 --> 00:05:48,963
Remember that first round of a hundred and twenty-four million?

133
00:05:48,983 --> 00:05:52,237
This one is over five hundred times bigger.

134
00:05:52,257 --> 00:05:56,289
That's more than double February's price, in about three months.

135
00:05:56,309 --> 00:05:58,241
Which is fantastic news.

136
00:05:58,261 --> 00:06:05,065
And, as it turns out, the reason it lost eighty billion dollars.

137
00:06:05,085 --> 00:06:07,437
June first, twenty twenty-six.

138
00:06:07,457 --> 00:06:11,621
Anthropic sends its IPO paperwork to regulators, in secret.

139
00:06:11,641 --> 00:06:14,507
Eleven days later, somebody else goes first.

140
00:06:14,527 --> 00:06:18,079
SpaceX lists at a hundred and thirty-five dollars a share,

141
00:06:18,099 --> 00:06:20,631
worth one point seven seven trillion,

142
00:06:20,651 --> 00:06:24,023
with almost a third of the shares set aside for regular people.

143
00:06:24,043 --> 00:06:27,195
Day one, it closes at a hundred and sixty-one.

144
00:06:27,215 --> 00:06:29,827
Then it peaks at two hundred and eleven.

145
00:06:29,847 --> 00:06:32,831
By the middle of July, it's below where it started.

146
00:06:32,851 --> 00:06:35,195
By early August, a hundred and eight.

147
00:06:35,215 --> 00:06:38,507
In about six weeks, half the peak is gone.

148
00:06:38,527 --> 00:06:41,822
That's the chart Anthropic's bankers get to look at every morning.

149
00:06:41,842 --> 00:06:43,994
In October, the books leak.

150
00:06:44,014 --> 00:06:47,498
Then, on October twenty-sixth, the filing goes public.

151
00:06:47,518 --> 00:06:50,190
Its ticker will be ANT.

152
00:06:50,210 --> 00:06:54,634
Sales in the first half of the year: sixteen point three billion dollars,

153
00:06:54,654 --> 00:06:57,946
up from about one point two billion a year earlier.

154
00:06:57,966 --> 00:06:59,930
Loss: eighty point seven billion.

155
00:06:59,950 --> 00:07:04,323
The draft's list of things that could go wrong ran ninety-four pages.

156
00:07:04,343 --> 00:07:07,438
We're worth more than twice what we were in February.

157
00:07:07,458 --> 00:07:08,210
Correct.

158
00:07:08,230 --> 00:07:10,306
Congratulations.

159
00:07:10,326 --> 00:07:14,518
Then why does this say we lost seventy-two billion dollars?

160
00:07:14,538 --> 00:07:17,884
Because you're worth more than twice what you were in February.

161
00:07:17,904 --> 00:07:19,576
Remember the IOUs?

162
00:07:19,596 --> 00:07:20,376
Amazon,

163
00:07:20,396 --> 00:07:22,616
Google and other early backers gave

164
00:07:22,636 --> 00:07:25,908
Anthropic cash in exchange for shares later.

165
00:07:25,928 --> 00:07:27,300
Picture a lemonade stand.

166
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:30,980
Your neighbor gives you a hundred dollars today for a slice of the stand,

167
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:32,792
handed over down the road.

168
00:07:32,812 --> 00:07:36,204
Then your stand becomes the hottest stand in America.

169
00:07:36,224 --> 00:07:40,008
That slice is now worth a fortune, and you still owe it.

170
00:07:40,028 --> 00:07:44,404
The accounting rules say: the more your promise is worth, the more you owe.

171
00:07:44,424 --> 00:07:46,910
And that jump goes on the books as a loss.

172
00:07:46,930 --> 00:07:49,442
Out of the eighty point seven billion,

173
00:07:49,462 --> 00:07:52,634
seventy-two point four billion is exactly that.

174
00:07:52,654 --> 00:07:54,439
A charge on paper.

175
00:07:54,459 --> 00:07:57,035
So did any actual money leave?

176
00:07:57,055 --> 00:07:58,259
Not for that part.

177
00:07:58,279 --> 00:08:00,991
Look only at the business of selling Claude,

178
00:08:01,011 --> 00:08:04,963
and it lost about seven hundred million dollars in those six months.

179
00:08:04,983 --> 00:08:06,195
That's real money.

180
00:08:06,215 --> 00:08:08,947
It's also a long way from eighty billion.

181
00:08:08,967 --> 00:08:11,187
And over the first nine months of the year,

182
00:08:11,207 --> 00:08:15,987
the business itself brought in six point six billion dollars more than it cost to

183
00:08:16,007 --> 00:08:16,471
run.

184
00:08:16,491 --> 00:08:17,743
Now flip it around.

185
00:08:17,763 --> 00:08:20,535
Amazon owns a pile of those IOUs.

186
00:08:20,555 --> 00:08:25,487
On its own books, it reported gains of more than sixty billion dollars from them.

187
00:08:25,507 --> 00:08:26,459
Same promise.

188
00:08:26,479 --> 00:08:28,271
One company calls it a loss.

189
00:08:28,291 --> 00:08:29,830
The other calls it a profit.

190
00:08:29,850 --> 00:08:31,262
And it keeps going.

191
00:08:31,282 --> 00:08:32,582
On November second,

192
00:08:32,602 --> 00:08:37,314
a new filing: the loss for the first nine months is a hundred and four billion

193
00:08:37,334 --> 00:08:38,066
dollars.

194
00:08:38,086 --> 00:08:41,878
That's more than SpaceX raised in its record IPO in June.

195
00:08:41,898 --> 00:08:44,310
Ninety-four billion of it is paper,

196
00:08:44,330 --> 00:08:48,922
including a two-billion-dollar charge for matching the staff's donations to charity.

197
00:08:48,942 --> 00:08:49,482
Meanwhile,

198
00:08:49,502 --> 00:08:54,174
Anthropic is sitting on about a hundred and thirty-two billion dollars in cash.

199
00:08:54,194 --> 00:08:55,486
More than it lost.

200
00:08:55,506 --> 00:08:56,588
Both numbers are true.

201
00:08:56,608 --> 00:08:59,148
So the scary number on the thumbnail is

202
00:08:59,168 --> 00:09:01,988
mostly a scoreboard of how fast Anthropic's

203
00:09:02,008 --> 00:09:02,760
value went up.

204
00:09:02,780 --> 00:09:03,623
Case closed?

205
00:09:03,643 --> 00:09:04,795
Not quite.

206
00:09:04,815 --> 00:09:08,187
Because buried in the same filing is a different number.

207
00:09:08,207 --> 00:09:12,149
And that one is real.

208
00:09:12,169 --> 00:09:15,301
Eight hundred and ninety-five billion dollars.

209
00:09:15,321 --> 00:09:19,473
That's how much Anthropic has signed up to pay for computers in the years ahead.

210
00:09:19,493 --> 00:09:22,557
Its sales so far this year: thirty-three billion.

211
00:09:22,577 --> 00:09:25,567
The bill is about twenty-seven times bigger.

212
00:09:25,587 --> 00:09:26,839
Here's who gets paid.

213
00:09:26,859 --> 00:09:29,663
Broadcom, about a hundred and sixty billion.

214
00:09:29,683 --> 00:09:31,415
Google, a hundred and twenty.

215
00:09:31,435 --> 00:09:33,607
Amazon, a hundred and ten.

216
00:09:33,627 --> 00:09:35,399
Microsoft, forty-five.

217
00:09:35,419 --> 00:09:39,451
And about a hundred and twenty billion to SpaceX's AI business.

218
00:09:39,471 --> 00:09:41,283
Yes, that SpaceX.

219
00:09:41,303 --> 00:09:46,554
The company whose IPO record Anthropic is chasing is also one of its landlords.

220
00:09:46,574 --> 00:09:48,354
Some of it can be cancelled,

221
00:09:48,374 --> 00:09:52,358
or depends on conditions: about two hundred and fourteen billion.

222
00:09:52,378 --> 00:09:55,550
But seventy billion is due in the next twelve months,

223
00:09:55,570 --> 00:09:59,163
and almost four hundred and fifty billion before twenty thirty-one.

224
00:09:59,183 --> 00:10:02,095
Why sign for so much, so far ahead?

225
00:10:02,115 --> 00:10:04,947
Because compute gets rented years in advance.

226
00:10:04,967 --> 00:10:07,859
Book too little, and you pay the last-minute price.

227
00:10:07,879 --> 00:10:09,931
That's exactly what happened this summer.

228
00:10:09,951 --> 00:10:15,843
From July to September, computers cost Anthropic nine point nine billion dollars,

229
00:10:15,863 --> 00:10:17,795
up seventy-seven percent.

230
00:10:17,815 --> 00:10:21,907
Five billion of that was expensive capacity grabbed at short notice.

231
00:10:21,927 --> 00:10:25,155
The business slipped back into a small operating loss.

232
00:10:25,175 --> 00:10:26,435
We need more computers.

233
00:10:26,455 --> 00:10:26,951
Today.

234
00:10:26,971 --> 00:10:28,999
Today costs more.

235
00:10:29,019 --> 00:10:30,605
How much have you got?

236
00:10:30,625 --> 00:10:33,529
About five billion dollars' worth.

237
00:10:33,549 --> 00:10:38,233
So that's one way to get it wrong: buy too little, and you pay through the nose.

238
00:10:38,253 --> 00:10:42,663
The other way is to buy too much.

239
00:10:42,683 --> 00:10:44,155
Which brings us to you.

240
00:10:44,175 --> 00:10:46,787
Here's how a company sells itself to the public.

241
00:10:46,807 --> 00:10:48,291
First, the filing.

242
00:10:48,311 --> 00:10:50,803
Then the roadshow: for about a week,

243
00:10:50,823 --> 00:10:54,095
bankers pitch the company to the biggest funds on Earth.

244
00:10:54,115 --> 00:10:57,959
The funds place orders: this many shares, at this price.

245
00:10:57,979 --> 00:10:59,759
The night before trading starts,

246
00:10:59,779 --> 00:11:03,191
the company and its banks pick the price that fills the book.

247
00:11:03,211 --> 00:11:07,618
Then the market opens, and the price does whatever it wants.

248
00:11:07,638 --> 00:11:09,069
How much did it lose?

249
00:11:09,089 --> 00:11:10,690
Which loss?

250
00:11:10,710 --> 00:11:11,850
The big one.

251
00:11:11,870 --> 00:11:14,166
Oh, that one's good news.

252
00:11:14,186 --> 00:11:16,269
Then we'll pay the low end.

253
00:11:16,289 --> 00:11:20,821
Goldman Sachs and Morgan Stanley lead it, with twenty-two banks in all.

254
00:11:20,841 --> 00:11:25,125
One more thing to know: Anthropic only gets the money from new shares.

255
00:11:25,145 --> 00:11:29,217
When early holders sell their shares in the deal, that money goes to them.

256
00:11:29,237 --> 00:11:32,249
And most insiders can't sell for six months anyway.

257
00:11:32,269 --> 00:11:34,545
The founders, for up to about a year.

258
00:11:34,565 --> 00:11:37,177
Regular people get a real seat this time.

259
00:11:37,197 --> 00:11:41,901
The filing names six brokers, from Schwab and Fidelity to Robinhood.

260
00:11:41,921 --> 00:11:45,933
